# Review — `docs/design/dao.lease.md`, round 4

All five scope documents read in full. Quotations of `dao.jing.md`, `dao.data.btree.md`, `dao.jing.dht.md`, and every `dao.stream.md`/`dao.stream.ws.md` citation were verified against the repo. The "no key to any DaoStream result map, `dao.stream.md` unchanged" claim holds. The Gray & Cheriton → Network Objects → RMI DGC → Jini lineage, the reference-counting-with-decay branch assignment, and the "traverses nothing, collects no cycles" fence rail are now correct.

## Findings, most severe first

**HIGH | Out of scope / Cancellation, 795–799 | The claim that non-renewal is "cooperative cancellation … in the way this document already asks holders to stop acting at their bound … and it needs no separate mechanism."** The cited discipline belongs to the wrong party. The holder's rule (320–327) tells a holder to stop acting **on what it holds** — it cancels the holder's *grant*, never the holder. When the thing being cancelled is itself an interpreter, that interpreter is either (a) the **leased resource** (the served-connection composition, the paused forwarder), to which the vocabulary gives no tenure-checking rule at all — it is not a holder of the lease over itself, and the judge's tolerance, which defines when tenure actually ends, is judge-private and unreadable from outside; or (b) a **renewing holder**, whose own bound never fires (it keeps renewing), so cancelling it is the grantor's policy lapse plus a medium-specific signal — possessor-actuated, not cooperative, and not non-renewal. Correction: keep the first half (holder non-renewal cancels the grant; stronger than a flag because it needs no live setter — sound) and delete or rewrite the second: state that cancelling the *holder* is either the possessor's reclaim act, or a composition-supplied rule in which the cancelled interpreter observes the lease over itself at its own points and stops at its own margin — a rule this vocabulary does not give it, which is why "needs no separate mechanism" is false.

**HIGH | Judging lapse / three limits, 597–608, with 542–570 | "In all three cases `:dao.lease/max` still bounds tenure, because tenure start is set once at the grant and no gap or restart moves it."** The paragraph contradicts its own limits one and two, which say every restart *resets* the cap measurement and that restarting faster than one duration extends a capped lease indefinitely — precisely because a restart *loses* tenure start unless the terms are persisted. Only limit three (gaps, no restart) preserves the ledger. Compounding it, the state-loss bullet (565–566) lists "a gap spanning the watched window" alongside restart as losing state, contradicting both the ledger-is-private-not-stream-derived claim (542 ff.) and the closing sentence's gap clause: a medium gap destroys *evidence*, not the judge's state. Correction: delete "or a gap spanning the watched window" from the state-loss bullet (gaps lose evidence; restarts lose state); replace the closing sentence with: "A gap alone never moves tenure start — the ledger is judge-private. A restart loses it unless the terms are persisted, which is why limits one and two are limits."

**HIGH | Judgment pass, step 2, 411–414, against 261–267, 505–506, 536–540 | "Drains the lease-fact cursor … stamping every fact it finds" is executable as written and wrong when executed.** Two defects. First, the medium is unnamed: the document itself says grant-facts and holder-facts "need not share a medium" (served-connection case: grant on the grantor's log, renewals on the deposit medium), yet the pass assumes one lease-fact cursor — it must say the cursor is on the medium carrying **holder-authored evidence**. Second, "every fact" includes the delivery copy of the judge's own `:accepted`, which the composition "carries … to the holder's medium as ordinary payload" (262–266). A literal implementer counts that copy as a "relevant fact — the grant or the newest renewal" (505–506) and re-seeds the interval window in whichever pass reads it, extending tenure by the medium's write-to-read lag, nondeterministically (the copy may have been evicted — 544–546). The seeding rule ("from its own act of granting, not from reading the grant back," 539–540) implies the exclusion; the pass never states it. Correction: step 2 reads "stamping every **holder-authored** fact it finds"; name the cursor's medium; state that grantor-authored facts establish nothing at the drain (the ledger already carries them from the act of authoring).

**HIGH | Judgment pass, step 2, 415–418 | "The stamp can only overstate a fact's recency."** Checkably false, and the falsity is licensed by the document's own premise that "nothing orders them in the medium" (413–414). Step 1 drains ticks deposited *before step 1*; a renewal deposited between step 1 and step 2 is stamped with the older tick reading — recency **understated** by up to one tick interval plus intra-pass skew, which errs toward false lapse, not retention. Correction: the stamp overstates recency without bound for older facts (retention direction) and understates it by at most one tick interval plus intra-pass skew (false-lapse direction); require that bound inside the tolerance budget in *Whose clock decides*, and delete "only." As written, the sentence props up the decision to stamp with pass-time rather than per-fact observation on a safety claim it does not have.

**MEDIUM | Ledger exit rule / pending-reclaim set, 526–531 with 456–458 | The round-3 exit rule relocated its leak.** The ledger now exits when reclaim is performed *and* the record lands; but a lease whose `:lapsed` append terminally fails — the grantor's own log closed, or permanently full — never exits pending-reclaim, the append is retried forever, and the set grows without bound: verbatim "the leak in the one component this document exists to keep bounded" (530–531). The fix is cheap because the reader rule already makes dropping safe ("the absence of a `:lapsed` fact is never evidence of tenure," 457–458, 592–593). Correction: bound the retry — after N attempts, or on a terminal append outcome, drop the entry and let the reader rule carry it; say so.

**MEDIUM | Judging lapse / Whose obligation / What a lease may never do — 547–548, 626, 667 vs 521–524 | Tracing-vocabulary residue: the round-3 denial is retracted in three later uses.** 521–524 states the ledger "is not a root set in the collector's sense"; 547–548 then calls the persisted terms "the judge's **own root set**," 626 says "the **root set** survives what the process does not," and 667 names the ledger "a grantor's own **root set**" (in a negation that still affirms the term). Also "under the mapping below" (523) dangles — no mapping below maps this. Under this review's own standard ("a residue of the wrong branch is worse than the original error, because the correction advertises accuracy"), this is the flagship residue. Correction: "the judge's persisted ledger" at 547 and 626, "a grantor's own ledger" at 667; fix or delete "the mapping below." (Two unadorned uses of "conservative," 71 and 554, should take the round-3 expansion "erring toward retention, without guaranteeing it" or a cross-reference to it.)

**MEDIUM | Out of scope / Cancellation, 792–795 | "A holder that stops renewing has said exactly that."** The document's own taxonomy forbids this: statuses are **speech acts** (244), `:lapsed` is a **conclusion** (367), and silence is "the one thing a dead holder reliably produces — the **signal**," not a saying (147–149). A dead or partitioned holder said nothing; its silence *counts as* cancellation in effect. The sentence sells the false lapse ("cancels when the canceller … is partitioned away") as unqualified strength, when it is the accepted spurious-cancellation cost that *Whose clock decides* works hard to survive. Correction: "a holder that stops renewing has *effected* exactly that — non-renewal is cancellation by default, the absence of continued wanting rather than a statement of it, and it can fire on a canceller that still wants continuation; the tolerance and renewal fraction are the price of that."

**MEDIUM | Renewal discipline, 320–327 vs 695–697 and 302–310 | The holder's bound omits the cap, and policy lapse leaves a renewing holder with no stop signal at all.** The grant carries `:dao.lease/max` to the holder (table, 181; delivery, 261–267), and "an honest long-lived holder proposes anew" past the cap — but the stated discipline bounds the holder only by duration since last renewal or grant observation. A cap-lapsed (or policy-lapsed) holder is by definition one that kept renewing; its stated bound never fires; it learns only from a medium-specific signal the vocabulary assigns to no one. Correction: extend "Stop acting at the bound" to "whichever comes first: the duration since its last renewal, or `:dao.lease/max` since it observed the grant — it holds both numbers"; and state plainly that for a policy lapse there is no holder-computable bound, the holder's remedy being the resource's own signal, exactly as with exclusion (332–335).

**MEDIUM | Attribution, 351–360 vs 633–645 | "Assembly refuses a judge handed no resolver, **or one incompatible with the medium it was given**, exactly as the WebSocket boundary refuses a reject-mode destination."** The analogy holds only for the absence case. The WS refusal works because retention mode is *declared nature* — data inspectable at assembly; a resolver is behavior, and "incompatible" has no declared basis to inspect. Two sections later the unwired driver is held to the honest standard — "detectable by review, with no assembly-time or runtime signature" (643–645). Correction: either require the resolver to carry a declared shape of what it resolves against (data, matching the analogy), or downgrade the incompatibility clause to review-detectable and keep assembly refusal for absence only. The document should not claim two enforcement strengths for one class of wiring defect.

**LOW | Cancellation, 801–807 | "A lease is the wrong instrument for a one-shot answer that will never arrive, where there is no continuation to end."** Misdrawn under Axiom 4 — waiting *is* a continuation in this system, and there are two in play: the waiter's (ended trivially, by dropping it) and, where the request holds server-side state, the per-connection composition — which is canonical use #2 and *is* lease-cancelled. The true boundary is directional: a lease ends only a **grant** — what its holder holds — and the awaiter holds nothing from the answerer. Correction: replace the continuation clause with the directional one.

**LOW | Duration representation, 212–220 | Zero magnitude is unclassified; "negative" is a dead word.** The representation admits a non-negative integer (zero is not in the defective list, which names only zero *entries*), yet "Durations are positive: absent, zero, or negative is not a grant" — so a zero-duration grant is neither defective nor a grant, and every judge invents its own handling; "negative" cannot occur under "non-negative integer magnitude." Correction: add zero magnitude to the defective list; delete "negative."

**LOW | Judgment pass, step 1, 408–410 | `now` is undefined for a pass that drains no tick.** The first pass races the first tick; a stalled tick stream yields passes with no reading. Correction: one sentence — a pass that drains no tick reuses the previous `now`; a judge with no `now` yet judges nothing.

**LOW | Ledger exit rule, 527–529 | The disjunct "or the reclaim that answered a release completed and recorded" is subsumed** by step 5's "`:lapsed` in every case … silence, release, cap, or policy" (426–430). Residue of the pre-round-4 design. Correction: collapse to "`:lapsed` appended."

**LOW | Lapse is a conclusion, 437–441 | "the one act the governing document allows a boundary to take beyond appending."** `dao.stream.md` licenses the boundary's self-close specifically for "a boundary that **can no longer deposit**" — a deposit-failure trigger, not a judgment trigger. The judge's close is ordinary host composition acting on what it possesses and needs no borrowed license. Correction: rest on possession alone, or mark the citation as analogy.

**LOW | A gap is not silence, 556–564 | The justification is mooted by its own assembly refusal.** The paragraph defends "do not infer that a surviving older renewal is the newest" via arbitrary eviction order, then requires judged media to declare evict-oldest — under which the scenario is impossible — and reserves the conservative rule "for a medium whose order is merely unknown," which cannot be a conforming judged medium. Correction: say whom the backstop protects — a judge wired to a medium whose declared nature was never checked, the same review-only class as the unwired driver — or cut the mooted justification.

## Severity statement

No Critical findings. Nothing violates the governing axioms or invariants; nothing touches `dao.stream.md`; the WebSocket citations are accurate. The GC framing is now substantively correct, and the tracing vocabulary is purged everywhere except the root-set family above. The HIGH findings are all local — one new section and two edit-seams — none structural.

## Is this document finishable?

Yes — but only if the next round is a *convergence* round, not a content round. Say it plainly: three rounds of fixes have each found the previous round's fixes wanting, and round 4 continued the pattern — six of the fifteen findings above (pending-reclaim exit, root-set residue, cap omission in the holder bound, the three-limits seam, the subsumed disjunct, the silence-as-speech erosion) are defects **created or left behind by round-3 and round-4 fixes at their citing sites**. That is a trend, not a tail, as long as rounds keep adding prose.

But it is a diagnosable trend, and the diagnosis matters: every defect is one of exactly two shapes. Either an **edit-seam** — a fix landed at its home site and not at the sites that cite the fixed thing — or **rhetorical overreach** — a safety or strength claim ("only," "exactly as," "needs no separate mechanism," "has said exactly that") extending one step past the mechanism that earns it. The specification core — the fact table, the attribution duty, the pass skeleton, the restart rules — is sound and close. The accretion is in the argumentation: the precedent is argued three times, the reader rule three times, the retention-direction four times; 815 lines is 60% growth carrying perhaps 15% new obligation.

What would stop it: (1) **split the document** — an operative contract (vocabulary, duties, pass, limits) from a design-rationale ADR (the JIT/GC lineage, the divergences, the Jini critique); the uncheckable claims live and multiply in the rationale. (2) A **terminology single-source** — every load-bearing term (root set, conservative, state, tenure) defined once and grep-checked after every edit; every "only/never/exactly as" backed by a named mechanism or downgraded. (3) A **freeze on new generalizations** for one round — the cancellation section is the first *scope expansion* in four rounds, and it is the source of the worst new finding; a document that opens new fronts while seams are open does not converge.

With those, one more round closes it. Without them, round 5 will find round 4 wanting, and round 6 round 5, indefinitely — correctly.
